[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The committed, human-reviewed strictdoc contents are the canon

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-CANON-NAMES-THE-COMMITTED-CONTENTS
TITLE: The committed, human-reviewed strictdoc contents are the canon
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: The beads graph and the canon merge into one store, or the canon stops being the reviewed, committed one.
STATEMENT: >>>
The body of .sdoc nodes committed to this repository -- durable, reviewed by a
human through a render, ratified by merge -- is THE CANON. "Graph" is not used
for it.

The operator's harness keeps a second, structurally similar graph in beads:
the LLM's working space, private, held in a separate repository, one per user
per repository, never crossing repositories, offered as a devenv module so
others can run the same system. The two stores are distinguished by
AUTHORITY, not by shape: the canon is what has been reviewed and committed;
beads is where work happens before, and around, that.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
"Graph" named the data structure, and both stores are graphs, so it
distinguished nothing. Candidates: canon, record, corpus. Canon won on naming
the property that separates the stores -- ratified, durable -- rather than the
shape they share. Record collides with decision records and with the
measurement notes nodes carry; corpus is in wide use here and costs nothing to
keep, but says nothing about review.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled by the operator on 2026-08-28. Existing nodes and code say "graph" and
"corpus" throughout; under INV-SETTLED-STAYS-VISIBLE they are READ as canon
and are not rewritten by this decision. The beads side is not modelled in the
canon at all and this node is the only place it is described. AUTHORED_BY
reads llm because the writer has no flag for the other value.
<<<
